 <a id="ctxsidebarright"></a>
 ## `ctx.sidebarRight`
 
+`holdRendering(sessionId)` keeps a Session view alive and renders its background sidebar behind the application without changing selection, focus, or active-tab semantics. Release the returned idempotent disposer when automation finishes; plugin shutdown releases outstanding holds. Background rendering is inert and hidden from accessibility navigation, including when the docked panel is collapsed.
+
 `openResource(address, options?)` and `openTab(kind, options?)` are the navigation controller, and every way into the column calls one of them: the conversation's file links and a tool row's line reference (`openResource(fileAddress, { params: { line } })`), the strip's add control and a guide entry box (`openTab`), a file tree's rows (`tab.actions.openResource`). A resource address is a `dsh-resource://<type>/…` URI; without `options.kind` the registry claims it (globs and `canOpen`, best band wins), with it that kind's type in force opens it. A page is named by kind; the tab is recorded under an address this package composes and nobody else spells (`contract/seed.ts`). Both run the same steps as one history entry: a resource tab already showing the same (kind, contentId) is focused wherever it sits unless `revealIfOpened: false`; ordinary page tabs always deduplicate within the target pane, regardless of that option; otherwise a new tab takes the place of `options.replaceTab` (closing that tab, described below), else in `options.paneId`, else in the active docked pane, which is the first docked pane while a floating pane is active. The panel expands, because content the user cannot see is not opened, unless the new tab replaces a floating one or the open reveals a tab in a floating panel. A reveal of a tab that was already on screen — the active tab of a floating panel, or of a docked pane while the column is expanded — makes the pane holding it glow once for about 600ms, whatever opened it and whatever type the tab is; providers do nothing for it. A reveal that switches to a background tab or expands the column does not glow, because that change is already visible. A docked pane glows inward, above its content's sticky bars such as code banners; a floating panel glows outward over its own shadow. The glow uses the accent colour in light mode and white in dark mode. A repeated reveal restarts the glow, the pane carries `data-sidebar-right-flash` while it shows, and with reduced motion the glow holds steady for the same time instead of fading. Each reveal plays once and only while its Session is on screen: hiding the Session ends a glow in progress, a reveal made while it is hidden is dropped rather than played on return, and a Sidebar mounted later does not replay an earlier reveal. Then the Tab domain records the navigation — `params` reach the body as `navigation.params`, with `revision` stepped — outside the layout history. `params` is typed by what is opened: a viewer for a resource type merges its entry into `SidebarRightResourceParamsMap` (the text preview declares `{ line?: number }`); a page type that takes parameters merges into `SidebarRightTabParamsMap` under its kind; values are JSON-shaped by convention, unchecked at run time. An address outside `dsh-resource://`, one no type claims, or a kind nothing registered throws: that is a wiring mistake, not a user error.
 
 `preferNewPane: true` first asks the target docked pane to split under the normal two-pane and room rules, then falls back to that pane when no split is available. `replaceTab` takes precedence and disables this preference.
